<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ur-Nammu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 2112–2094 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/22nd-century-bce">22nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ur-Nammu was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a>, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire</a>, who established his rule around 2112 BCE after the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a>. He is credited with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/code-of-ur-nammu">Code of Ur-Nammu</a>, one of the oldest known law codes, and with major building projects including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat-of-ur">ziggurat of Ur</a>. Accounts of the Neo-Sumerian Empire treat him as the architect of the state's foundation, governance and legacy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ur-nammu.jpg" alt="Ur-Nammu" width="320" height="276" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Steve Harris, source · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Ur-Nammu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a>, Ur-Nammu reigned for 18 years; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/year-name">year-names</a> are known for 17 of them, though their order is uncertain. One records the devastation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gutium">Gutium</a>, and two seem to commemorate his legal reforms, among them the year in which he made justice in the land. His military exploits included the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a> and the defeat of his former masters at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a>. He was recognised as a regional ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, Eridu and Uruk at a coronation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a>, and he is believed to have built at Nippur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a>, Adab and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a>. He restored the roads and general order after the Gutian period.</p><p>His inscriptions report the defeat of a coalition of the Elamite ruler Kutik-Insusinak and cities including Tutub and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a>. He ordered the construction of several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurats</a>, including the Great Ziggurat of Ur. He held the titles King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> and King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a>, and his personal goddess was Ninsuna. In royal hymns he and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shulgi">Shulgi</a> call <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugalbanda">Lugalbanda</a> and Ninsun their holy parents and describe themselves as brothers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilgamesh">Gilgamesh</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Death of Ur-Nammu, a later Sumerian composition, describes his death, funeral and passage through the underworld. Its account of the death is damaged, vague and metaphoric, though some scholars have read it as saying that he died in battle at the hands of his own troops. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shulgi">Shulgi</a> succeeded him.</p><p>Many scholars hold that Ur-Nammu was one of the few Mesopotamian kings of the third millennium BCE who was not deified after death, yet he was worshipped: the palace at Tummal included funerary chapels for him and his wife, and he may have been buried there. The Ur-Nammu Stela, found by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonard-woolley">Leonard Woolley</a> in the temple precinct of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanna">Nanna</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> in the 1920s, survives in 106 fragments and is held at the University Museum of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ur-Nammu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ur-Baba's daughters married three later rulers, Gudea, Nam-mahani and Ur-gar, and he appointed Enanepada high priestess of Ur, an office Naram-Sin had filled before him and <b>Ur-Nammu</b> would fill after.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lagash">Lagash · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ur-Nammu</b>, who governed Ur under Utu-hegal and may have been his kinsman, soon took power in his own right and founded a new dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 21st century BCE Utu-hengal of Uruk drove the Gutians out of southern Babylonia; on his death his governor at Ur, <b>Ur-Nammu</b>, moved the kingship there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After Utu-hengal expelled the Gutians, power passed almost at once to <b>Ur-Nammu of Ur</b>, probably his brother or a governor he had appointed, who founded the Third Dynasty of Ur; Uruk was honoured as a cult centre but governed from Ur.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uruk">Uruk · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The code of <b>Ur-Nammu</b>, founder of the Third Dynasty of Ur, around 2100 BCE, is the oldest known, three centuries older than that of Hammurabi, and the code of Lipit-Ishtar of Isin followed in the 20th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/language/sumerian">Sumerian · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>After Akkad's collapse Puzur-Inshushinak expanded as far as Eshnunna, but he was driven back by <b>Ur-Nammu</b> around 2100 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/awan-dynasty">Awan dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/shulgi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/shulgi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Shulgi</span><span class="tp">Child &#183; r. c. 2094–2047 BCE</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
